05,0.30451683]\nSelect 4 from the remaining? But we have 6 from previous round; do we select only among those? Possibly we retain only selected indices from previous round, then from those we select again. So first round selects 6 indices. Then second round only considers those 6 indices and selects 4 from them based on their second round scores.\n\nIf that's the case, then retained_indices might be the final set of indices after all rounds (i.e., the ones selected in the last round). History would be list of selections per round.\n\nSo for first example, first round selection: indices [0,1,3,4,5,7] (if we choose highest 6 scores). Second round: we consider only those indices, look at their second round scores:\n- index0: 0.66498626\n- index1: 0.36035978\n- index3: 0.01113057\n- index4: 0.18849451\n- index5: 0.17335601\n- index7: 0.30451683\nWe need to select 4. Highest scores: index0 (0.665), index1 (0.360), index7 (0.305), index4 (0.188). So indices [0,1,7,4] (or sorted by index). That would give retained_indices = [0,1,4,7]? But wait forced=[4] so index4 is forced to be selected? It was selected in first round, but in second round it is not forced? The forced list is given per payload, maybe it applies to first round only? In first example forced=[4] means index4 must be selected in first round. In second example forced=[2] means index2 must be selected in first round. In third forced=[3].\n\nSo first round selection must include forced indices.\n\nNow we need to verify if this matches expected output? We don't have expected outputs, only fixtures. But we can test by implementing algorithm and see if it's plausible. Also note that history should be list of lists of integers. In first round selection we choose 6 indices; second round we choose 4 indices from those 6. So history = [[selected indices round0], [selected indices round1]]. retained_indices = selected indices of last round.\n\nBut we need to be sure about ordering: The history list order? Probably round